This diff is clean and matches the spec/design. Implementation is complete.

## Handoff

**Changed files:**
- `frontend/src/App.jsx` — added `pendingDeleteIds` state + `pendingDeleteTimers` ref (a `Map<id, timeoutHandle>`), `UNDO_WINDOW_MS = 5000` constant, `deferDelete(id)` and `cancelPendingDelete(id)`. `activeTasks` (tasks minus pending-delete ids) now feeds `stateFiltered`/`completedCount`/`openCount` instead of raw `tasks`. `TaskList`'s `onDelete` now points at `deferDelete` instead of `run(() => api.deleteTask(id))`. `<UndoToast>` mounted unconditionally as the last child of `<main>`, fed `pendingDeleteIds.map(id => tasks.find(t => t.id === id))`.
- `frontend/src/components/UndoToast.jsx` — new. Returns `null` when `pending` is empty; otherwise renders a `role="status"` stack of toast rows (`"{title}" deleted` + Undo button + decorative `aria-hidden` progress bar driven by the same `windowMs` prop as the real timer).
- `frontend/src/App.css` — new `.undo-toast*` rules per `docs/design.md`'s styling table (fixed bottom-center, `#16191c`/white, ellipsis title, custom button states, CSS-only shrinking progress bar via `@keyframes undo-toast-shrink`).
- No changes to `backend/`, `frontend/src/api.js`, or `frontend/src/main.jsx`, per spec.

**Key implementation notes:**
- AC6 (silent 404 on expiry): `api.js`'s `request()` throws `Error(detail?.detail || ...)` with no status code attached, and `api.js` is explicitly out of scope to touch. I matched on the exact string `"no such task"` — the literal `detail` FastAPI sends for this route's 404 (`backend/app/main.py:69-70`) — to decide whether to swallow the error. This is a string-coupling to backend wording; if that detail message ever changes, this silent-swallow breaks. Flagging it since it's the one spot where "reuse `api.js` unmodified" and "distinguish 404 from other errors" pull in slightly different directions.
- On a genuine (non-404) failure of the deferred delete, the pending entry is still removed (matching the spec's "no stale button" edge case) but `refresh()` is *not* called, so the un-mutated task simply reappears — same pattern `run()` already uses elsewhere when a mutation fails.

